<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nazi concentration camps</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1933">1933</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Konzentrationslager</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> were a system of more than a thousand camps and subcamps that Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> built and ran in Germany and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> between 1933 and 1945. The first were set up in March 1933 after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became Chancellor; from 1934 the SS ran them. Communists were the first prisoners, then habitual criminals, asocials, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and, after the war began, people from across occupied Europe. Prisoners were held in inhumane conditions and died of disease, malnutrition, forced labour, maltreatment and execution: of about 1.65 million registered prisoners, about a million died. From 1941 a linked system of extermination camps was built for mass murder.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Nazis had no plan for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> before they took power. The system arose in the months after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> became chancellor on 30 January 1933, from the wish to suppress tens of thousands of opponents. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> fire in February was the pretext for mass arrests, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> allowed detention without trial. The first camp, Nohra, was set up on 3 March 1933 in a school. About 70 camps opened that year in any convenient building, run by local police, the SS, the SA or state ministries, and about eighty per cent of the prisoners were members of the Communist Party.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Theodor Eicke, appointed commandant of Dachau in June 1933, drafted a Disciplinary and Penal Code, and Dachau became the model for other camps. After the purge of the SA on 30 June 1934 the SS took over the remaining camps. Arrests of people labelled habitual criminals or asocials tripled the prisoner population to 24,000 by June 1938. In the war the camps grew more brutal, and prisoner numbers rose from 21,000 in August 1939 to about 70,000 to 80,000 in early 1942. Most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> murdered in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> were never held in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a>, since most of those killed by gas died in separate extermination camps.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Writers count 27 main camps and, by Nikolaus Wachsmann's estimate, more than 1,100 satellite camps. Before the war most prisoners were Germans, but by its end only 5 to 10 per cent were from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and treatment depended largely on nationality; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, Slavic prisoners and Spanish Republicans suffered especially harsh treatment. Forced labour in quarries and brickworks, and later in war production, was a constant feature. Major evacuations in 1944 and 1945 killed prisoners in large numbers on death marches, and the camps were liberated gradually. About 1.65 million people were registered as prisoners, and nearly a million died.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nazi concentration camps” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From May to June 1944 Hungarian authorities rounded up hundreds of thousands of Jews and sent them to <b>Nazi concentration camps</b>, where most died; in northern Transylvania many Jews who had welcomed Hungarian rule in 1940 were deported by Hungarian gendarmes to Auschwitz-Birkenau.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
